def cps(s: str) -> str:
return " ".join(f"U+{ord(c):04X}" for c in s)
print(cps("— ·"), "·".encode("utf-8").hex(" ")) # U+2014 U+0020 U+00B7 c2 b7
Trick: when text looks wrong, stop guessing and read the bytes. A code-point dump such as cps() shows exactly what a string holds, whatever font or console you have. Two strings that look the same but dump differently are the bug.

Search for the two fingerprints: U+7E5A anywhere, and a double ? outside code fences. (JavaScript's nullish operator is two question marks too, so matches inside code are fine.)
from pathlib import Path
MARK = chr(0x7E5A) # CP950 reading of the UTF-8 bytes for "·"
FENCE = "`" * 3 # a Markdown code fence
def scan(path: Path) -> list[str]:
hits, in_code = [], False
for n, line in enumerate(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines(), 1):
if line.lstrip().startswith(FENCE):
in_code = not in_code
continue
if MARK in line:
hits.append(f"{path.name}:{n}: U+7E5A")
if not in_code and "??" in line:
hits.append(f"{path.name}:{n}: double ? outside code")
return hits
for p in sorted(Path("content/posts").glob("*.md")):
for hit in scan(p):
print(hit)

Change katex: true to katex: false in every .md file of a folder, without changing the encoding.
Solution
$utf8NoBom = New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)
Get-ChildItem -Path .\content\posts -Filter *.md | ForEach-Object {
$t = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($_.FullName, [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)
$new = $t -replace '(?m)^katex: true(?=\r?$)', 'katex: false'
if ($new -ne $t) {
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($_.FullName, $new, $utf8NoBom)
}
}
$_.FullName is already a full path. The lookahead (?=\r?$) leaves any \r in place, so line endings do not change either (see Dev Essentials 04 · Line Endings). Then check in Git Bash: grep -l $'\xe7\xb9\x9a' content/posts/*.md should print nothing, and git diff --stat should list only the files you meant to change.
